From 7ed09a25bc6273f28c96b955918d6135ed17d41e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michael Spanier <spanier.michael@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 22:09:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 05/34] usb_webcam: assemble UVC frames from checked payload
 headers

UVCDeframer::Write() read the payload header without checking it: an
empty packet read past its end, a header length larger than the packet
gave a negative payload size, and the error bit was ignored, so broken
frames were shown. When the frame ID changed without an end-of-frame
bit, the first payload of the new frame was still added to the old one.
When the queue was full, the new data was dropped instead of the old
frame.

The header is now parsed once in uvc_parse_payload_header() (new
UVCPayload.h, no Haiku dependencies, so it can be tested on its own):
too short packets and impossible header lengths are skipped. A frame
ends with the end-of-frame bit or before the first payload with a new
frame ID. A frame with the error bit, an empty frame and the first
frame after the start, which usually begins in the middle, are dropped.
When the consumer is behind, the oldest queued frame goes.

With more than one isochronous transfer in flight, which larger sizes
need, the packets can be handed over out of order. The SOF counter in
the source clock field of the header shows that: a step back marks the
frame as broken instead of showing it torn.

UVCPayloadTest covers the header parsing, the frame checks and the SOF
order with headers as the built-in camera of a ThinkPad T470s sends
them.

Signed-off-by: Michael Spanier <spanier.michael@gmail.com>
Assisted-by: claude-opus-5-5
(cherry picked from commit 5f1a56a62368ef80ed14d89c376c77bd27c0d2b6)
